/* Game G4 of Beyene, Chaudhuri, Popeea, Rybalchenko, "A constraint-based approach to solving games on
infinite graphs", POPL 2014, pp. 221-233, https://doi.org/10.1145/2535838.2535860
(Table 1; Section 5.3), as the paper defines it: Eve = Cinderella, Adam = Stepmother (moving first),
capacity c = 1.4, objective win(0) = GF p0 (infinitely often no overflow), where win(i) = GF p_i /\ AND_{j<i} FG not p_j, i.e. the least
colour seen infinitely often is i.  Every state (after either player's move) carries a colour.
Cinderella-Stepmother game, Section 2 of the paper: five buckets b1..b5 on a circle, all empty
initially.  stepmother(v,v') = (b1'+..+b5' = b1+..+b5+1 /\ bi' >= bi): she pours one unit, split as she
likes.  cinderella(v,v') = some i with bi' = 0 /\ b(i+1 mod 5)' = 0, the other buckets unchanged.
overflow(v) = some bi > c.  The game is turn-based with Adam moving first (Section 3.1), Eve's
deadlock losing and Adam's deadlock leaving no play; the game does not stop at an overflow.
Bucket contents are reals (the paper's capacities 1.4 and its strategy's 0.8, 0.1 are not integers).
The objective is a min-parity condition, solved by the nested fixpoint nu X0. mu X1. ... with one
equation per colour and position kind, colour 0 outermost (the first equation is the outermost);
X_i's sign is nu when i is a colour Cinderella wants least, mu otherwise.  A_i(v): Stepmother to
move, E_i(v): Cinderella to move.
Colours: 0 = no overflow (p0), 1 = overflow; Cinderella wins iff colour 0 recurs.  Expected verdict from Table 1.
expected: valid */
